WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.900
Many machine knitters
complain about their stash.

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.740
"I have so much yarn,
I'll never use it in this lifetime."

3
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.580
Well, put it to use.

4
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:16.580
There are people out there who could
use knitted items that you can make.

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.800
Dust off that machine, use up that yarn,
and get a feeling of satisfaction

6
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:27.260
for making something
for someone who's in need.

7
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.320
Plan on learning techniques and practicing

8
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.900
the skills that you need
to improve your knitting.

9
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:39.120
Here are six ways that you can
knit for charity and help others.

10
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.120
Knitting blankets or lap robes is

11
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.580
a wonderful way to provide warmth
and comfort to those in need.

12
00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.480
Think about preemies and newborns,

13
00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:54.180
the elderly, those undergoing chemo,
or homeless shelters.

14
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:59.580
Animal shelters also provide
blankets for our furry friends.

15
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.740
Consider washable,
soft yarns for blankets.

16
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.850
But the sky's the limit in using
color and stitch patterns.

17
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.460
A really easy blanket is
our Right Angles Blanket.

18
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.290
This is a great exercise
in learning about gauge.

19
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.620
Knit a swatch

20
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:21.620
and let our pattern determine the stitches
and rows for your blanket size.

21
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.680
We even include a tool to help you

22
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.620
determine if you have enough
yarn for the size you've chosen.

23
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.660
Knit four to six squares,

24
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:34.900
attach them together (as
you knit) for minimal finishing.

25
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.480
Susanna's Blanket adds the challenge

26
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:42.140
of short row shaping
for its interesting design.

27
00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:47.400
It's a fun one to knit,
especially in self-striping yarns.

28
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.620
Knitted hats are always in demand,
especially during the colder months.

29
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.140
You can donate them to homeless shelters,

30
00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.100
hospitals, or organizations
that support cancer patients.

31
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.290
Or you can knit for premature babies.

32
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.170
Experiment with different patterns
and yarns to create a variety of styles.

33
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.540
Hats can just be plain old rectangles,
easy to knit.

34
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.960
With our Mile-a-Minute Hat project,

35
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:20.380
you cast on one time and you can knit a
handful of hats in one knitting session.

36
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.540
Once they're off the machine,

37
00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:28.120
seam up and finish the hat in front
of the TV or out on the deck.

38
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.340
Maybe you'll challenge yourself to shape
the top of a hat for great fit.

39
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.960
Dust off your ribber and knit
some hats in the round.

40
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.730
Any hat with a fold up brim will fit

41
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.640
almost anyone, and the doubled
brim makes for warmer ears.

42
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.060
If you are in the US,

43
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:57.740
let me remind you of Linda Williams' hat
contest at Country Knitting of Maine.

44
00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:02.860
Another quick and easy and useful
project are soap sacks.

45
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.980
These are washcloth pouches that can be
practical to donate and a thoughtful gift.

46
00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:14.200
They hold bars of soap and double
as exfoliating washcloths.

47
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.640
Donate them to homeless shelters or

48
00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:21.400
organizations that offer
hygiene items to those in need.

49
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:22.840
Stacey at soapsacks.

50
00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:28.340
Com inspired me to put all those bits
and pieces of cotton yarn to good use.

51
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.240
Plus I've had so much fun knitting

52
00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:36.500
a simple 4 inch by 6 inch project,
knowing that I'm helping someone in need.

53
00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.320
How about some more rectangles?

54
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:42.380
Scarfs and shawls can
provide warmth and comfort.

55
00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:47.340
Consider knitting various lengths,
colors, and stitch patterns.

56
00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.460
Donate them to homeless shelters,

57
00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:56.360
women's shelters, or organizations
that support people facing cold weather.

58
00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.740
I've discovered that when
my neck is warm, I'm warm.

59
00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:04.580
Maybe a needy person's coat
or jacket isn't very warm.

60
00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:12.080
A scarf can add extra comfort in cold
weather, and you can help them stay warm.

61
00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.660
If you've graduated past a plain old
rectangle, consider our butterfly shawl.

62
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.920
The shaping is easy and it adds a bit

63
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:25.080
of interest to the knitting
and to the finished shawl.

64
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.620
Here's another reason to dust off
that ribber or garter carriage.

65
00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:36.640
Play with stitch patterns and sizes and
use our Rectangle Square tool to calculate

66
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.500
how many stitches to cast
on and how many rows to knit.

67
00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.800
Use our Yarn Calculator to determine if

68
00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:48.460
you have enough yarn to
knit the size you've chosen.

69
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.340
Are you ready for a challenge?

70
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.020
How about mittens?

71
00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.980
Handknitted mittens are
welcome during the winter.

72
00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:00.200
Choose soft and insulating
yarns to keep hands cozy.

73
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:06.560
Look for organizations that collect winter
accessories for children or the homeless.

74
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.860
Our mitten collection has five
styles and eight customizable sizes.

75
00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:17.000
You can create easy to knit,
comfortable mittens.

76
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.060
Are you feeling extra ambitious?

77
00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.900
Maybe you're looking to learn or
practice some new-to-you techniques.

78
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.220
Use up that yarn with our easiest

79
00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:32.460
and fastest sweater pattern the
Kid's Squirrel Cardigan.

80
00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.540
If you can cast on and bind off,
then you can knit this.

81
00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.600
It's knit in one piece
with just two side seams.

82
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:46.060
Another one-piece quickie is our
two hour child and infant cardigan.

83
00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.860
This one gives you good short row
practice and can be finished in no time.

84
00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.140
Don't be afraid to knit sweaters.

85
00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.500
Will they be perfect?

86
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:55.740
Probably not.

87
00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.900
Will they be judged at the county fair?
No.

88
00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.700
The recipient will be happy to have

89
00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.660
something warm to slip
on that was made with love.

90
00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.540
I encourage you to use up that yarn,

91
00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:10.780
put your machine to use,
and knit for charity.

92
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.040
You'll feel great helping others.
Happy knitting.

